  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nLatest radar imagery reveals that there are some showers, and a few \nembedded thunderstorms moving up over northern Baja California at \nthis hour, with a few of these showers beginning to move over \nportions of SE San Diego County. This will continue to move up from \nthe south in the form of a lot of altocumulus and altostratus, with \nas few showers, and some of these may make it through the drier PBL \nand reach the ground. With this in mind, the marine layer has been \ndisrupted a bit with the presence of the mid-level clouds passing \noverhead. Any bases that have struggled to form have generally \nbeen based around 1000-1500 MSL. There may also be some rain drops\nfelt, even through the low level clouds, due to the convective \nsubtropical mid-level clouds moving overhead. \n\nWhen analyzing the near-storm environment on SPC, it is evident that \nthe majority significant instability remains confined over the \ndeserts, with the highest values of MUCAPE (although it is well-\ncapped). Since there is some elevated instability associated with \nthis monsoonal wave as it transitions up over San Diego County this \nmorning, it may likely produce a few rain showers, and possibly even \na rumble of thunder can he heard all the way down to the coastal \nareas. Depending on how much it clears out by later this morning \nwill determine whether or not it will warm enough to get \nthunderstorm development over our mountains by later this afternoon. \nAs this moisture moves out by tomorrow with more southwesterly flow \npredominantly taking over in the mid levels, it will allow for more \ndrier conditions to settle back in for tomorrow with likely little \nto no cloud development over the mountains for Monday.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...\n\nFrom tuesday into Wednesday, the ridge will weaken slightly a shift \na little further eastward with slightly better troughing influence \nupstream which will allow for temperatures to be slightly cooler, \nand only around 5 degrees above the seasonal average by Wednesday. \nThe drier air advection in the mid levels out of the southwest will \nhelp to hinder any afternoon monsoonal development. Then, as the \nridge begins to build a bit and expand/retrograde back towards the \nwest beginning Thursday, it will allow for temperatures to \ngradually warm again up to around 10 to 15 degrees above the \nseasonal average, especially by Friday. There will also be an \ninflux of mid-level moisture again out of the southeast as these \noriginate over Arizona and Sonora, Mexico, and then transition \nover our region. The ridge will begin to slowly weaken and flatten\na bit going into Saturday, translating to only slightly cooler \ntemperatures to begin the following weekend, although still quite \nhot... even for summertime.",
